# Working with GodotLiveMCP in this project

This project has the GodotLiveMCP bridge addon enabled — the Godot editor
is live and reachable through MCP tools while it's open. A few things that
make this work well, learned from real use:

## Save at checkpoints, not on every call

Every `eval_expression`/`set_property`/`add_node_live`/etc. call only
changes the running editor's **in-memory** scene. Nothing reaches disk
until `save_scene_live` is called (or the user saves manually). Closing or
reloading the editor without saving silently discards all of it.

- Call `save_scene_live` after finishing each meaningful step of a task —
  not after every trivial property tweak (wasteful, and can itself trigger
  Godot's file-watcher).
- If a step also involves writing or editing a `.gd` script file directly
  (normal Read/Write/Edit — that's correct, scripts are files, not bridge
  state), be aware Godot may prompt "reload from disk?" if it detects that
  external file change while the scene itself has unsaved bridge edits.
  Saving the scene doesn't prevent that prompt (it's a different Godot
  code path — external file-watch, not in-memory scene state) but keeps
  the *scene* changes safe regardless of how the user answers it.

## eval_expression vs. a structured tool

- Prefer a structured tool when one exists for the operation — it's
  schema-validated and usually cheaper in round trips than hand-writing
  GDScript.
- Use `eval_expression` for one-offs, reads, or anything reachable as a
  single method call (no assignment, no loop needed) — GDScript's
  `Expression` class can't parse assignment statements or run loops, so
  multi-step or looping operations need a structured tool, not eval.
- `eval_expression` has direct access to `ProjectSettings`, `ClassDB`,
  `Engine`, `Input`, `OS`, `Time`, `Performance`, `AudioServer`, and
  (in-editor) `EditorInterface` as bare identifiers — no need to look up a
  dedicated tool for anything reachable through those singletons in one
  call, e.g. `ProjectSettings.get_setting("physics/2d/default_gravity")`.

## Verify property changes, don't trust ok:true blindly

`set_property`/`set_properties` and every resource-configuring tool
(`setup_collision`, `set_material_3d`, `setup_environment`,
`set_physics_material`, `set_theme_stylebox_override`,
`add_audio_bus_effect`, `set_particle_material`) validate property names
and return a readback of what was actually applied — trust that readback,
not just `ok: true`. If a property name is wrong, the call now fails
loudly with "unknown property/properties: ..." rather than silently doing
nothing (this was a real bug: an agent recolored a sphere with the wrong
property name, got `ok: true`, and nothing changed).

To assign an *existing* texture or resource file (not a freshly-created
one), use a value of the form `"load:res://path/to/file"` — this resolves
via `load()` instead of trying to parse the string as a Variant literal,
e.g. `{"albedo_texture": "load:res://player_skin.png"}`.

**Never use a colon-path property name** like
`"material_override:albedo_texture"` — that is not a real property name
and will fail (or, before this was fixed, could silently corrupt the
parent property instead). Resolve the underlying resource
(`node.material_override`, `node.mesh`, etc.) and target its own property
directly instead — via a structured tool if one creates/configures that
resource type, or via `eval_expression` for a plain-value read.

## Self-improvement rule

If a request required falling back to `eval_expression` for something the
structured tools don't cover, and the pattern looks likely to recur (not a
one-off), say so and suggest promoting it into a dedicated tool in
GodotLiveMCP — don't build it silently into this project, and don't ask
after every single prompt, only when a repeatable gap is actually observed.
